#
# SCENARIO 2 -- two g5.8xlarge over EFA, DP=2, maximum memory occupancy.
#
# Source of truth for both reproduction paths: `source` it for the direct-EC2
# path, and the same values appear in kustomization.yaml for the Kubernetes
# path. g5/scenario-check.sh asserts the two agree.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------- MEASURED
# What a 2-node run over EFA has actually been measured to do, 2026-10-04,
# 1000 iterations on real c4 (g5/results/run-2node-20261004-221909-rank1.log):
#
#   transport     "Selected provider is efa" on both ranks
#   throughput    25,056 tok/s median, stdev 460 (1.8%), 99 steady-state steps
#   step time     0.654 s at 16,384 tok/step
#   stability     loss 11.9155 -> 5.5249, 0 skipped, 0 NaN
#
# THAT MEASUREMENT USED THE 4-LAYER `smoke` GEOMETRY, not the one below. It
# peaked at 4.83 GiB -- 21% of the card -- so it validates the EFA path and the
# throughput, and says nothing about memory occupancy. To reproduce it exactly:
#
#   NUM_LAYERS=4 HIDDEN_SIZE=1024 FFN_HIDDEN_SIZE=3072 \
#     NUM_ATTENTION_HEADS=8 NUM_QUERY_GROUPS=2 \
#     GLOBAL_BATCH_SIZE=16 TRAIN_ITERS=1000 ./g5/finish-run-2node.sh
#
# ------------------------------------------------- WHY THIS GEOMETRY INSTEAD
# This scenario asks for maximum memory, so it uses the ~1B geometry -- the
# same shape measured at 19.08 GiB (84.8%) on ONE node -- with DP=2.
#
# Per-rank memory should be LOWER than that 19.08 GiB, not higher, for a
# reason worth stating because it is counter-intuitive: g5/train.py enables
# use_distributed_optimizer when WORLD_SIZE > 1, which SHARDS optimizer state
# across the data-parallel group. At 1.009 B parameters the Adam moments plus
# fp32 master weights are ~12 B/param = ~11.3 GiB, so DP=2 removes ~5.6 GiB
# per rank. Activations are unchanged: GLOBAL_BATCH_SIZE=16 at DP=2 gives each
# rank 8 sequences, exactly what the single node processes at GBS=8.
#
# So the estimate is ~13.4 GiB per rank with ~9 GiB headroom.
#
# THIS COMBINATION IS NOT MEASURED. The number above is arithmetic from two
# measurements (the 1B static state and its activation residual), not a
# reading. It is also why SEQ_LENGTH stays at 1024 here: 2048 at this geometry
# OOMed on ONE node (g5/results/run-20261003-104814.log, 20.05 GiB allocated
# with 38 MiB free), and while the sharding above should make it fit at DP=2,
# stacking two predictions is not a default. See the README for that test.
NUM_LAYERS=20
HIDDEN_SIZE=1536
FFN_HIDDEN_SIZE=4608
NUM_ATTENTION_HEADS=12
NUM_QUERY_GROUPS=3
SEQ_LENGTH=1024
MICRO_BATCH_SIZE=1
# 16 = 8 sequences per rank at DP=2, matching the single-node per-rank load.
GLOBAL_BATCH_SIZE=16
TRAIN_ITERS=1000
LOG_INTERVAL=10
NNODES=2
# NOT read by finish-run-2node.sh or run.sh. On the EC2 path USE_EFA is a
# PROVISIONING knob, consumed by g5/launch-instance.sh (which attaches the
# interface and sets the security-group shape) and g5/fix-cluster-sg.sh. The run
# itself AUTODETECTS the fabric from /dev/infiniband, because the device is
# either present or it is not and a flag could only disagree with the hardware.
# So source this file BEFORE launching, not just before running -- the README's
# recipe does. On Kubernetes it IS read at run time, by the pod script.
#
# Not optional at 2 nodes. Without an EFA device this model measured 8,086
# tok/s against 24,727 on ONE node -- a 3x LOSS -- because the step degenerates
# to the 1,033 MB gradient transfer at 4 Gbit/s. See g5/README.md.
USE_EFA=1
